From: tokenscope — https://github.com/PS12007/tokenscope
Subject: [null control, do not merge] mul_mat_id: the same one-line change, on a path a dense model never enters

This is not a proposal. It is the control arm for FINDINGS F33's open question
(audit issue M6), and it exists to be measured and then reverted.

The problem it addresses. `patches/03-mulmat-chunk-threshold.patch` changes one
constant in `ggml_compute_forward_mul_mat` and measures +1.62% [+1.10, +2.15] on
decode. But the stock and patched binaries differ by 1,137,994 bytes spanning
most of the image -- one constant changed and a quarter of the file moved, almost
all of it downstream address shift. Code layout is therefore NOT held constant
between F33's two arms, and layout alone can move throughput by around a percent
on this kind of workload. Nothing in that design can separate the two.

What this does. It applies the identical edit -- `nth * 4` -> `nth * 2` -- to
`ggml_compute_forward_mul_mat_id` instead, at the same file's line ~1698, and
leaves `mul_mat` at stock. `mul_mat_id` is the MoE expert path. **A dense model
never executes it**, so for `mid.gguf`, Qwen2.5, Qwen3-8B or any other model in
this repo the patched binary must behave identically to stock, while its
compiler and linker inputs are perturbed in the same file, in the same function
shape, by the same kind of edit.

How to read the result, against stock, same protocol as F33
(`ab_throughput.py --blocks 3`, both arms uninstrumented, rebuilt together):

  - interval contains zero  -> layout is not worth a percent here, and F33's
                               +1.62% is attributable to the scheduler change
  - interval excludes zero  -> a behaviour-free edit to this file moves decode,
                               so an unknown part of F33's number is layout and
                               the finding needs restating, not just caveating

The second outcome is the informative one and is the reason to run it.

MEASURED AFTER WRITING THE ABOVE, and it changes what this patch is for
(FINDINGS F38). This edit perturbs **5 bytes** of the binary -- one of code,
four of build metadata. The compiler emitted the same instruction with a
different immediate and nothing moved. Patch 03's edit, in `mul_mat`, perturbs
**1,137,994 bytes**, a contiguous ~1.1 MB block inside `.text` at ~94% density,
which is about 31% of the whole code section.

So this is **not** a layout control and must not be described as one. The same
one-byte immediate change is layout-neutral in one function and moves a third of
the image in another; layout perturbation is a property of where the edit lands
and cannot be dialled in by choosing a small edit.

What this patch actually provides is a **null control**: two binaries that must
behave identically on any dense model AND are laid out identically. Measured
against stock with the same protocol, it estimates the harness's false-positive
rate, which nothing in this project has ever measured. A non-zero result here
means the harness reports differences between binaries that cannot differ.

--- a/ggml/src/ggml-cpu/ggml-cpu.c
+++ b/ggml/src/ggml-cpu/ggml-cpu.c
@@ -1695,7 +1695,7 @@
         int64_t nchunk0 = (nr0 + chunk_size - 1) / chunk_size;
         int64_t nchunk1 = (nr1 + chunk_size - 1) / chunk_size;
 
-        if (nchunk0 * nchunk1 < nth * 4 || disable_chunking) {
+        if (nchunk0 * nchunk1 < nth * 2 || disable_chunking) {
             nchunk0 = nr0 > nr1 ? nth : 1;
             nchunk1 = nr0 > nr1 ? 1 : nth;
         }
